        "final_message": "The implementation of `maximum(xs)` is now complete and verified. Here's a summary of the work:\n\n**Task:** Implement `maximum(xs)` with the empty-input convention (return `0` for an empty list).\n\n**Solution:** The function checks if the input list `xs` is empty. If it is, it returns `0`. Otherwise, it returns the maximum value using Python's built-in `max()` function.\n\n**Code in `solution.py`:**\n```python\ndef maximum(xs):\n    if not xs:\n        return 0\n    return max(xs)\n```\n\n**Verification:**\n- Empty list `[]` \u2192 returns `0`\n- `[1]` \u2192 returns `1`\n- `[1, 2, 3]` \u2192 returns `3`\n- `[-1, -2, -3]` \u2192 returns `-1`\n\nThe implementation satisfies the requirement to handle the empty-list case by returning `0`, as established by the earlier lesson in memory (the empty-input convention).\n\nThe work is complete. No further action is needed.",
